[ceiling] These are the inside horizontal return and end face of the blade's R6 upper shoulder, at Z 83.00 and Z 80.00. The exterior YZ round and the orthogonal XZ shoulder remain exposed for the 0.08/0.12/0.24 layer-height comparisons. Automatic supports are retained in the prepared finish project; both X sides and the open interior admit removal. The reference does not claim physical support removal or transferred appearance acceptance.
alternatives: A solid upper head adds backing but changes the inward shoulder and its cooling section. A sloped internal return removes the flat ceiling while changing the curved thin-wall reference. Printing this shoulder as a separate bed-facing part removes the current overhang/orientation comparison.
file: hardware/printed-parts/calibration/soda-studies/parts/a08-angle-and-curve-blade.step
click: x=0.000 y=-4.606 z=83.000
click: x=0.000 y=-13.500 z=80.000

[ceiling] These are the blind underside storage sockets: diameter 6.30 mm, roof Z 2.25, mating the 6.00 mm carrier pegs of height 2.00 mm. The 0.15 mm radial air and 0.25 mm top air keep these gravity-parked samples removable. Their bed-facing openings provide removal access; the saved project retains its automatic support decisions. This describes nominal docking geometry, not measured release force or roof strength.
alternatives: An open through-bore eliminates the socket roof but cuts the sample's upper face or active comparison surface. A 3.00 mm roof requires 2.25 mm more local stock above the current 3.00 mm heel; it raises or enlarges the heel and needs new display placements. A shallower peg preserves a thicker roof but reduces the two-millimetre storage engagement.
file: hardware/printed-parts/calibration/soda-studies/parts/a08-angle-and-curve-blade.step
click: x=0.000 y=-3.000 z=2.250
click: x=0.000 y=-15.000 z=2.250

[slope] This is the intentional 45-degree inner underside of the stepped YZ angle blade, running beside its X-facing side wall. The outside ramp values progress from 0 to 90 degrees in 15-degree steps and the three blades share this geometry. The prepared finish projects retain automatic support decisions for this underside; the active outside ramp is not replaced with a support-free form. Physical appearance remains unrecorded.
alternatives: A solid filled blade or a transverse supporting rib grounds the underside but changes the specimen's section and thermal exposure. Sloping it in X changes its manufacturing azimuth. A separately printed ramp face avoids this underside while giving up the continuous angle-and-round sample.
file: hardware/printed-parts/calibration/soda-studies/parts/a08-angle-and-curve-blade.step
